    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my materials class and im a bit confused. why do we actually need to put steel rebar inside concrete for foundations? i get that concrete is strong, but whats the deal with the relationship between its compressive strength and the tensile strength of the steel? like why do they need each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question! Concrete is excellent in compression, but it’s brittle in tension—it cracks and snaps under pulling forces, like when a foundation settles unevenly or soil pushes sideways. Steel rebar, on the other hand, is ductile and handles tension beautifully. So they’re a perfect pair: concrete takes the squeezing, steel takes the stretching. Together, they form a composite that resists both forces, which is why foundations don’t just crumble or tear apart.",
      "source": "target",
